# Testing Standards

> file_patterns: ["*_test.py", "test_*.py", "*_tests/*.py"]
> description: Guidelines for test organization and implementation

## Test Organization
Standards for organizing test files

- Test directories should be named ./xxx_tests
- Organize test files by module name
- Create necessary __init__.py files
- Group related tests in test classes
- Separate unit tests from integration tests

## Test Implementation

### Framework
> required: true

Use pytest and its plugins exclusively:
- Use pytest.mark for test categorization
- Use pytest fixtures for setup/teardown
- Use pytest.raises for exception testing
- Use pytest-cov for coverage reporting

### Documentation
> required: true

All tests must have type annotations and documentation:
- Document test purpose and expectations
- Include type hints for test functions
- Document fixture usage and requirements
- Explain complex test scenarios

### Test Utilities
Best practices for test utilities:
- Use fixtures for resource reuse
- Use parametrize for test parameterization
- Use mock for external dependency simulation
- Create helper functions for common operations

## Test Entry Points
Guidelines for test entry points:
- Simple tests in __main__
- Complex tests in pytest
- Provide quick validation through __main__
- Include example usage in __main__

## Test Cases

### Coverage Requirements
- Test all public interfaces
- Test edge cases and error conditions
- Test both success and failure paths
- Maintain minimum 80% code coverage

### Test Quality
- Tests should be independent
- Tests should be deterministic
- Tests should be fast
- Tests should be readable

### Test Data
- Use meaningful test data
- Avoid hardcoded test values
- Use factories for complex objects
- Clean up test data after use

## Validation Requirements
- All code must have corresponding tests
- Tests must be independent and repeatable
- Test coverage should be maintained
- Tests must be well-documented and maintainable 